The sum of the angle measures in a quadrilateral is always $360^\circ$, so we have  \[360=x+x+y+y=x+x+\frac{900}7+\frac{900}7.\]Therefore  \begin{align*}
x+x&=\frac{360\cdot7-900-900}7\\
&=\frac{180\cdot14-180\cdot5-180\cdot5}7\\
&=\frac{180\cdot4}7\\
&=\frac{720}7.\\
\end{align*}Since $x$ is half of that, $x=\boxed{\frac{360}7}$ degrees.

Place the ellipse in the coordinate plane, as usual, so that the center is at the origin.  Then the equation of the ellipse is
\[\frac{x^2}{25} + \frac{y^2}{16} = 1.\]Also, the distance from the center to each foci is $\sqrt{5^2 - 4^2} = 3,$ so one foci is at $F = (3,0).$

[asy]
